{% from "sidenote.njk" import sidenote %}
<p>
    Sometimes I'll come across a game that's hard to find information for, because
    the reviews helpfully say "don't read anything about this game before playing it" or
    <span class="spoiler">they're just big walls of spoiler tags</span>.
    Faced with a such a lack of key information, I immediately buy and download the game.
</p>
<p>
    Discussions that look like the Epstein files are a sign that the game is what I call an <em>epiphany-driven game</em>
    {%- call sidenote("sn-metroidbrainia") %}
    I have also seen the term "<a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/metroidbrainia/">Metroidbrainia</a>",
    which describes a Metroidvania where areas are unlocked through player knowledge rather than in-game abilities, 
    but I find that this term excludes too many games that evoke the same core feelings as Outer Wilds.
    For example, I find that Subnautica feels similar to Outer Wilds, 
    even though it has little to no knowledge gating at all.
    {% endcall -%}.
    These games reward curiosity with paradigm-shifting moments of understanding.
    It's like being able to go back in time to re-discover quantum physics.
    You can't read about these games until you've played them, because learning the game is the whole point.
    In my opinion, these are some of the best, most satisfying games in existence,
    but they can only be played once! For that reason, I am always seeking more.
</p>
<p>
    Like many others, my Epiphany Game addiction started with Outer Wilds.
    This game is simultaneously genre-defining and unique.
    A search for "games like Outer Wilds" finds dozens of
    <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/outerwilds/wiki/index/gamerecs/">posts from other players feeling
        a bit empty</a>
    after completing the game or its DLC. There really is nothing quite like Outer Wilds, but there are
    games that are fun for some of the same reasons. The core of an Epiphany Game is: 
    <strong>
        A coherent universe with hidden truths that must be discovered through self-guided exploration.
    </strong>
    For strong epiphanies, the worldbuilding must be solid, so that the revelations make sense,
    and the game must allow the player to choose their own lines of inquiry,
    so that the accomplishment feels earned.
</p>
<p>
    Here is a spoiler-free tier list of Epiphany Games I've completed.
    The tier criteria are how much I think the games fit the category, and how much I liked the game, combined and weighted on vibes.
</p>

